WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.360
Sometimes the greatest stories write themselves.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:05.820
James Chen: "Look the US has been chasing

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:09.920
a Street Fighter IV/ Street Fighter V
championship for quite some time.

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:14.640
But you know who else has been chasing
that this one man right here. We're

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.540
actually at a point right now where the
American could be the favorite here.

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:24.440
More EVO Top 8s probably than any other
player in the history of EVO. There are

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:29.120
so many powerful names that didn't make
it this far and this is the one player

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:32.820
he probably did not want to make it back
out of losers' bracket. This is it. This is

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:38.440
the biggest match of his life. The onus
is on Punk to really close this out.

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:43.440
This is where the mental fortitude comes into
play.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.180
It's all slipping away from him."

00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:57.100
Heading into EVO 2017, Hajime “Tokido” Taniguchi – a fighting game legend – was

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.340
entering year 10 of an EVO first place drought.

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:02.440
He had 21 Top 8 finishes,

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:08.120
including two titles in Capcom vs. SNK 2 and Super Street Fighter 2 Turbo, but

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:12.340
EVO’s headlining Street Fighter crown had always managed to escape his grasp.

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:17.420
Tokido was a dominant, innovative master of fighting games with dozens of

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:22.479
tournament wins across multiple titles.
This was most visibly on display at CEO

00:01:22.479 --> 00:01:27.649
2011 when he made history by placing
first in Street Fighter IV Marvel vs.

00:01:27.649 --> 00:01:31.580
Capcom 3 and BlazBlue and placed third
in Tekken 6

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:36.550
However, despite his tremendous achievements, two second place finishes in

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:42.020
2007 and 2013 were the closest he ever
came to claiming the most prestigious

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:45.830
title in fighting games. The EVO Street Fighter main event.

00:01:45.830 --> 00:01:51.530
Going into EVO 2017’s Street Fighter V tournament, Tokido had an incredibly hard

00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:56.330
road to make it to Top 8, including a showdown with the man everyone had pegged

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.180
as the favorite to win it all.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.580
Victor “Punk” Woodley, a young, rising superstar

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:04.920
from the United States with an
incredible tournament record.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:10.940
Punk had won or placed secnod at nearly every major
tournament leading up to EVO. He was a

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:15.530
wunderkind with a hyper aggressive play
style, a goofy over-the-top personality...

00:02:15.530 --> 00:02:19.380
Punk. "It's just another day."

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:23.820
...and an uncanny
ability to get in his opponents' heads.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:29.780
[Casting.]

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.420
UltraDavid: "Punk is probably best player in the world. That's my, that's my thought. I'll
be very surprised if he was not in Top 8."

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:40.380
Everyone was sure that Punk was going to win EVO. Not could, would.

00:02:40.380 --> 00:02:45.180
Punk looked certain to be North America's
first Street Fighter EVO champion in

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.620
years and it seemed like Tokido was the
only person left who could stop him.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:54.630
While Tokido’s 2017 had been shaky, heading into EVO, stories emerged that he

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.760
had begun to regain his form.

00:02:56.980 --> 00:03:06.380
[Casting]

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:11.819
Fword: "I've heard a lot of stories about how he's been performing this week
in the casuals. Some of your number one

00:03:11.820 --> 00:03:16.180
guys here had a really hard time against
Tokido so he could be a real problem this EVO.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:19.680
The two clashed in Top 32. The winner would go to Top 8

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.500
while the loser would have to fight their

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:25.480
way back through the losers' bracket with
only one more chance before heading home.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:57.020
[Casting]

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:00.020
Punk moved on, and Tokido, defeated,

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:01.800
was relegated to losers'.

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:08.459
But he'd been there before. Tokido is
one of Japan's five fighting game gods

00:04:08.459 --> 00:04:12.450
and at the beginning of his journey he
did it all with a look of cold

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:17.789
emotionless disdain. Earning him the
nickname Murderface. However as the years

00:04:17.789 --> 00:04:22.109
wore on he wanted to become a player
that was more fun to watch. He became

00:04:22.109 --> 00:04:23.560
known for his trash talk...

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:27.640
Tokido: "If he use Ryu, I body him."

00:04:28.180 --> 00:04:29.580
...his oppressive play...

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:37.220
[Casting]

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:40.920
...and his over-the-top displays of dominance.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:50.100
[Casting]

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:54.700
But without an EVO win in a decade
his legacy seemed incomplete he had

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:58.760
dedicated years of his life to being the
best. Years of his life to the Fighting

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:02.690
Game Community to being someone that
people would remember. He just needed

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:05.200
another chance to make it all count

00:05:08.420 --> 00:05:13.380
In the loser's bracket Tokido came up
against fellow Japanese fighting game god Haitani.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:18.040
James Chen: "It's terrifying that these
two have to fight each other to get into Top 8."

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.960
Their match came down to the
very last round but a huge dropped combo

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:25.880
from Haitani gave Tokido the chance to
take his place in Top 8.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:52.400
[Casting}

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.460
The next day Tokido played Filipino Champ

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.060
the best Dhalsim in the world.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.980
James Chen: "Filipino Champ right here probably one

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:04.540
of the greatest heels that we have in
the community

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.640
you either love the guy or you're gonna
hate the guy."

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:12.080
Champ took a 2-1 lead and reached a match point with a wild series of mixups.

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:33.020
[Casting]

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.620
But Tokido fought back.

00:06:41.540 --> 00:07:14.440
[Casting]

00:07:14.740 --> 00:07:17.760
It all came down to a final, fifth game.

00:07:23.320 --> 00:08:08.760
[Casting]

00:08:09.100 --> 00:08:14.160
Having narrowly escaped elimination, Tokido moved on to play NuckleDu – one of America’s finest

00:08:14.220 --> 00:08:15.620
and won again.

00:08:15.840 --> 00:08:27.560
[Casting]

00:08:27.740 --> 00:08:33.680
Next came Itabashi Zangief, the world’s best Zangief player, known for wild, unpredictable play

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:37.380
that tended to stump even experienced players like Tokido.

00:08:37.540 --> 00:08:39.980
The two traded games back and forth

00:08:40.300 --> 00:09:43.800
[Casting]

00:09:43.820 --> 00:09:49.200
But in the final round of their fifth game, Tokido came out on top with an unbelievable read.

00:09:49.360 --> 00:10:25.640
[Casting]

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:29.720
Next came Kazunoko, but Tokido was ready for every trick he had.

00:10:35.520 --> 00:11:08.900
[Casting]

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:13.660
After blazing a trail of destruction through a gauntlet of Street Fighter V’s best

00:11:13.660 --> 00:11:17.100
players, Tokido had finally earned another shot at Punk.

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:19.420
James Chen: "Look, the US has been

00:11:19.420 --> 00:11:23.880
chasing a Street Fighter IV/ Street Fighter V championship for quite some

00:11:23.890 --> 00:11:28.120
time but you know who else has been
chasing that this one man right here."

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:31.870
Punk made it through the winners' bracket, having 3-0’d both ItaZan and

00:11:31.870 --> 00:11:37.180
Kazunoko. He was poised to win it all, and bring down Tokido yet again in the

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:41.200
process. With the home crowd behind him, the rookie would defeat the veteran,

00:11:41.200 --> 00:11:44.180
writing his name in Street Fighter history.

00:11:45.760 --> 00:11:48.440
But Tokido was writing his own story.But Tokido was writing his own story.

00:11:55.640 --> 00:12:51.520
[Casting]

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:53.760
The second set wasn’t much better for Punk.

00:12:53.960 --> 00:13:56.980
[Casting]

00:13:57.140 --> 00:14:01.780
Punk had lost before, but never in front of a crowd like this. Never in front of

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:06.680
thousands of people and never on Street Fighter’s most prestigious stage.

00:14:06.820 --> 00:14:09.140
But Tokido destroyed him.

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:46.940
[Casting]

00:14:46.940 --> 00:14:53.940
Tokido stood victorious, and to cap it all off, he struck Akuma’s signature Raging Demon pose.

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:58.720
Sajam: "The post on the Evolution stage. Your champion Tokido."

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:02.600
Tokido didn’t need to hit Punk with a Raging Demon when he was pixels

00:15:02.600 --> 00:15:04.980
away from death. But he did it, anyway.

00:15:05.400 --> 00:15:09.620
[Casting]

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:12.520
He beat Punk in the flashiest way possible,

00:15:12.520 --> 00:15:17.500
getting the crowd on his side and crushing Punk’s spirit.

00:15:17.700 --> 00:15:21.960
But he didn’t do it for that purpose. Tokido did it all for the crowd.

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:26.560
He didn’t end with trash talk, he closed out EVO with a message of love for the

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:27.700
Fighting Game Community.

00:15:28.020 --> 00:15:30.340
Gootecks: "You know, you came all the way from Japan.

00:15:30.340 --> 00:15:34.240
Is there anybody at home or is there anybody that
you want to give a shout-out to?"

00:15:36.060 --> 00:15:37.100
Tokido: "Yes."

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:39.580
"Uh."

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:44.780
"Just one thing I want to say."

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:51.100
"Fighting game, is something so great."

00:15:52.020 --> 00:15:53.400
[Cheers]

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:54.760
Tokido: "Thank you so much."

00:15:55.620 --> 00:16:01.560
Tokido worked for decades to earn that EVO win.
He played dozens of games, beat hundreds

00:16:01.560 --> 00:16:06.460
of the best players on Earth, all to
stand at the peak once again.

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:11.640
But that trophy meant more than a win over Punk.
It meant more than experience and craft

00:16:11.650 --> 00:16:17.740
trumping raw skill. Tokido stood at the
top of a community of people, every last

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:20.000
one of them out to become better.

00:16:20.420 --> 00:16:22.680
James Chen: "I can
tell you right now. Everybody probably

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:27.160
felt the same way: if there was going to
be a Japanese player who was going to

00:16:27.160 --> 00:16:29.960
stop that run, they would've wanted it to be
Tokido.

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:32.060
In the end, esports aren’t always

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:36.840
about one team or player being better
than another. Those teams and players

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:42.550
come and go, glory fades. But what keeps
these games going, what gets you to play,

00:16:42.550 --> 00:16:47.140
what makes these moments so special, is
the community that's behind them.

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:53.300
It's the community that shapes, makes and
appreciates these perfect moments for what they are.

00:16:53.300 --> 00:16:55.380
Something so great.

00:16:55.580 --> 00:16:57.580
James Chen: "Damnit, why did he have to say that?"

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:02.440
[Crowd chants "Tokido"]

